Identifying potions isn't that hard.  You need a unicorn horn and a
random junk item (a stack of arrows/darts/bolts is nice).  Dip the
junk/missiles into the potion.  If they turn into something else, the
potion was polymorph.  Yah, you just blew a conduct, but unless you're
playing a conduct game (which newbies shouldn't), big deal.  If the
items are missiles, and the potion is sickness, the items are now
poisoned missiles (which lawfuls shouldn't use, as you get an
alignment penalty).

If nothing interesting happens (or should I say, nothing other than
"Interesting..."), dip the unicorn horn into the potion.  If the
potion turns from one color to another, the original potion was
sickness, and the resulting potion is fruit juice.  If the potion
turns to water, the original potion was one of hallucination,
blindness, or confusion.  If the horn is coated with an oily sheen, it
was, well, oil...

If you have sleep resistance (sleeping), free action (paralysis) and
more than 15 (acid) or so HP, you can safely drink the potion now. 
There is a chance that the potion is invisibility which may be
permanent, but mummy wrappings are a decent temporary cure for that.

There are some other things that you can do to identify a potion, but
I find them less than useful.  An amethyst dipped into a potion of
booze will change it to water (or is it fruit juice... don't
remember).  A potion of acid dipped into a fountain, or other water
source (moat) will boil vigorously.  Poisoned items dipped into any
healing type potion will have the poison removed (but I find this is a
big waste... better to drink up instead!)

I've tried to look through potion.c for more examples, but I'm not
that familiar with that part of the code.  I may well have missed some
combinations (outside of alchemy) that allow you to identify an item
(and I intentionally omitted references to invisible objects).

